{"title":"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A convex polygon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f4008768812f46db4b252c9e69cf98c5faaa4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in the plane is dissected into smaller convex polygons by drawing all of its diagonals. The lengths of all sides and all diagonals of the polygon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f4008768812f46db4b252c9e69cf98c5faaa4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are rational numbers. Prove that the lengths of all sides of all polygons in the dissection are also rational numbers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f4008768812f46db4b252c9e69cf98c5faaa4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a triangle, the problem is trivial.  Otherwise, it is sufficient to prove that any two diagonals of the polygon cut each other into rational lengths.  Let two diagonals which intersect at a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> within the polygon be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is a convex quadrilateral with sides and diagonals of rational length, we consider it in isolation.\n</p><p>By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Law_of_Cosines\" title=\"Law of Cosines\">Law of Cosines</a>, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/7/117ac8894199b93371d6cfcdbb79410ba7ba6a2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos BAC = \\frac{AB^2 + CA^2 - BC^2}{2AB \\cdot CA}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"248\" height=\"39\" />, which is rational.  Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/7/d877c42e5d8ca49cbccf73d146c0546338b97e01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos CAD$\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" /> is rational, as well as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/6/3661ca97d9442831d287708bc92d65a62cebd109.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos BAD = \\cos (BAC + CAD) = \\cos BAC \\cos CAD - \\sin BAC \\sin CAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"572\" height=\"18\" />.  It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/0/5e017f4bb4e064305e671eb1b29536b7106d8019.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin BAC \\sin CAD$\" width=\"139\" height=\"13\" /> is rational.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/8/fc83ba0f4557ee11f38adeccb29f49c4baa319ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin^2 CAD = 1 - \\cos^2 CAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"209\" height=\"16\" /> is rational, this means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/e/53eb54d31345127e38ed1e84b9bdd4549d6310e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sin BAC \\sin CAD}{\\sin^2 CAD} = \\frac{\\sin BAC}{\\sin CAD} = \\frac{\\sin BAP}{\\sin PAD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"336\" height=\"39\" /> is rational.  This implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/c/80c2fbf8d6826cfac77dddec7f39210e1bc7a9d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{ AB \\sin BAP}{ AD \\sin PAD} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} AB \\cdot AP \\sin BAP}{\\frac{1}{2} AP \\cdot AD \\sin PAD} = \\frac{[BAP]}{[PAD]} = \\frac{BP}{PD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"423\" height=\"47\" /> is rational.  Define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to be equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/e/50e0bae92942e0ca1a7bd4f864ff205a2b9e25cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BP}{PD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"32\" height=\"37\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/e/50e0bae92942e0ca1a7bd4f864ff205a2b9e25cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{BP}{PD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"32\" height=\"37\" /> is rational; hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is rational. We also have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/2/632ad04c153f25501de0659e8e76530175047129.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1+r)PD = (1+\\frac{BP}{PD} )PD = PD + BP = BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"376\" height=\"37\" />, which is, of course, rational.  It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/7/f874b39870586f0504f50a667f2bafedddf47904.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/d/b9d6394023b7f71962bce796b61343dcc276679c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PD$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> both have rational length, as desired.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO\" title=\"2003 USAMO\">2003 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2003\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017202645\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.031 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.073 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 156/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1663/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4025/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,